 "sections": [
  {
   "id": "in-plain-english",
   "heading": "In plain English",
   "role": "intuition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "in-plain-english-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "The trace of a square matrix is just the sum of the numbers on its main diagonal. It sounds trivial, but it has two powerful properties: you can rotate the order of a product inside it without changing the answer, and for the special matrices used in regression it counts dimensions. That makes it the standard trick for computing averages of squared errors."
      },
      {
       "t": "callout",
       "kind": "why",
       "title": "Why it matters",
       "md": "It is where the N minus K degrees of freedom in the estimated error variance come from.",
       "folded": false
      }
     ],
     "words": 83,
     "symbols": []
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "definition",
   "heading": "Definition",
   "role": "definition",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "definition-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "\\mathrm{tr}(A) = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_{ii}"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "defined for square matrices."
      }
     ],
     "words": 4,
     "symbols": [
      "$A$, $B$, $C$",
      "$n$, $K$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "properties",
   "heading": "Properties",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "properties-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "table",
       "md": "| Property | Statement |\n| --- | --- |\n| Linear | $\\mathrm{tr}(A + B) = \\mathrm{tr}(A) + \\mathrm{tr}(B)$, $\\mathrm{tr}(cA) = c\\,\\mathrm{tr}(A)$ |\n| Transpose | $\\mathrm{tr}(A') = \\mathrm{tr}(A)$ |\n| **Cyclic** | $\\mathrm{tr}(ABC) = \\mathrm{tr}(BCA) = \\mathrm{tr}(CAB)$ — but **not** $\\mathrm{tr}(ACB)$ in general |\n| Scalar trick | A scalar is its own trace: $\\mathbf{x}'A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathrm{tr}(\\mathbf{x}'A\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathrm{tr}(A\\mathbf{x}\\mathbf{x}')$ |\n| Eigenvalues | $\\mathrm{tr}(A) = \\sum_i\\lambda_i$ |\n| Idempotent | If $AA = A$, $\\mathrm{tr}(A) = \\mathrm{rank}(A)$ |"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "**Example**: $\\mathrm{tr}\\begin{pmatrix}1&2\\\\3&4\\end{pmatrix} = 5$, and its eigenvalues are $5.372$ and $-0.372$, which sum to 5."
      }
     ],
     "words": 32,
     "symbols": [
      "$A$, $B$, $C$",
      "$\\lambda_i$",
      "$\\mathbf{x}$",
      "$c_1, \\ldots, c_k$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "the-expectation-of-a-quadratic-form-trick",
   "heading": "The expectation-of-a-quadratic-form trick",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "the-expectation-of-a-quadratic-form-trick-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "For a random vector $\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$ with $E[\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}'] = \\sigma^2\\mathbf{I}$ and a fixed matrix $\\mathbf{M}$:"
      },
      {
       "t": "math",
       "tex": "E[\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}'\\mathbf{M}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}] = E[\\mathrm{tr}(\\mathbf{M}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}')] = \\mathrm{tr}(\\mathbf{M}\\,E[\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}']) = \\sigma^2\\,\\mathrm{tr}(\\mathbf{M})"
      },
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "Three moves: scalar = its trace, cyclic property, trace and expectation commute (both linear)."
      }
     ],
     "words": 22,
     "symbols": [
      "$\\boldsymbol{\\varepsilon}$",
      "$\\sigma^2$",
      "$\\mathbf{M}$"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "common-mistakes",
   "heading": "Common mistakes",
   "role": "depth",
   "steps": [
    {
     "id": "common-mistakes-1",
     "blocks": [
      {
       "t": "prose",
       "md": "- Using the cyclic property to swap two inner factors: $\\mathrm{tr}(ABC)\\neq\\mathrm{tr}(ACB)$.\n- $\\mathrm{tr}(AB) \\neq \\mathrm{tr}(A)\\,\\mathrm{tr}(B)$."
      }
     ],
     "words": 11,
     "symbols": [
      "$A$, $B$, $C$"
     ]
    }
   ]
